Named contexts in `~/.config/zenctl/config.toml`. Filesystem only — no bus,
and `ZENKEY_EXPLORER_CONFIG_DIR` is what keeps this corpus out of yours.

```console
$ zenctl context --help
Manage named connection contexts (config file)

Usage: zenctl context <COMMAND>

Commands:
  create  Create (or update) a named context
  list    List contexts (the `*` marks the current one)
  show    Show one context (default: the current one)
  select  Select the current context
  rm      Remove a context
  edit    Open the whole config file in $VISUAL/$EDITOR, validating afterwards
  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

$ zenctl context create --help
Create (or update) a named context

Usage: zenctl context create [OPTIONS] <NAME>

Arguments:
  <NAME>
          

Options:
      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base this context pins

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --registry <DIR>
          Registry dir, repeatable (offline slice source)

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting for this context

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Default reply timeout in seconds

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file for this context (#122) — the passthrough that reaches a secured bus; explorer knobs apply on top (flag > env > context > file)

      --select
          Select it as the current context

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl context list --help
List contexts (the `*` marks the current one)

Usage: zenctl context list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl context show --help
Show one context (default: the current one).

`--format json` is how a CI job asks which deployment a runner is pinned to: `zenctl context show --format json | jq -r .base`.

Usage: zenctl context show [OPTIONS] [NAME]

Arguments:
  [NAME]
          

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

